WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:03.203
-My first guest is one of my
all-time favorite comedians.

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:05.738
He has a new book out
called "Is This Anything?"

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:09.075
which is filled with 45 years
worth of his stand-up material,

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:12.011
and it is so good, I mean, like,
the best stand-up material.

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:14.747
You can just read this, and even
if even if you're not funny,

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:16.716
you sound funny.
That's how good it is.

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.317
Before we say hello to Jerry,

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:21.587
please take a look at this
iconic moment from his career.

00:00:21.587 --> 00:00:23.856
Here he is throwing out
the first pitch

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:26.625
at Citi Field last year.

00:00:26.625 --> 00:00:28.727
-He does the Turk Wendell move,

00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:30.963
slamming the jacket down.

00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:32.465
Then with
the exaggerated wind-up

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.500
comes sidearm like Terry Leach

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.336
and throws a perfect strike.
-Very nice.

00:00:37.336 --> 00:00:41.207
-Here he is,
Jerry Seinfeld, everybody!

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.475
-Jimmy, I can't believe
you showed that.

00:00:43.475 --> 00:00:45.077
That's my favorite thing!

00:00:45.077 --> 00:00:47.480
-That's the thing.
I always mess up your intro.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.682
I figured I'd give you,
for Thanksgiving this year --

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:52.117
You're always on tradition,
but it's fun.

00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:53.919
You're always here
on Thanksgiving.

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:57.490
-Yes. I love always being
with you on Thanksgiving, on TV.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:00.359
'Cause we don't want
real Thanksgiving.

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:03.195
We want TV Thanksgiving.

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:04.831
-Yeah.
-Right? That's what we like.

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.065
-Yeah. We don't do the -- No.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:08.301
What would we do
at a real Thanksgiving? No.

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.369
-No. You got to laugh
at real Thanksgiving.

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:11.670
What the hell good is it?

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.573
-No, this is exactly --
-You want to get a laugh,

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:16.042
that means money in the bank.
-Thank you.

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:18.411
You know me so well.
[ Both laugh ]

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:19.846
I want to talk about the book.

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:22.781
But first, how is the family?
How's Jessica? How's Sascha?

00:01:22.781 --> 00:01:24.416
-Everyone --
-They're all good?

00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:26.686
-All good. Thank you for asking.
How about yours?

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.522
-They're great. They're cute.
It's awesome.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.157
But, I mean, my kids are --
They're little.

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:32.725
I mean, your kids are --

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.728
-They're still your kids
Don't diminish them

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:36.996
because they're young.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:38.330
-I know, but they won't
see this. I mean,

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.466
I'll show this to them
when they're older, you know?

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:42.902
They're 5 and 7 right now, so --

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.604
-Oh, that's nice.

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:46.905
-But your kids are 17.
They're teenagers. 20.

00:01:46.905 --> 00:01:49.375
-17, 15, and 20, yeah.

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:51.210
-Wow. 20 years old.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.313
How is it? How is it
with teenagers and older kids?

00:01:54.313 --> 00:01:55.848
-The greatest.

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:58.351
It only gets better.
Everything gets better.

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:03.589
And I like teenagers a lot
because of the hostility, the --

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:07.160
They're great debaters.
They will fight you.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:11.397
They will break down
your flaws in your logic.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.065
-Yeah.
-So it really

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:14.767
works better for me.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.202
Jessica was a great mom,

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:20.740
but I really want
to engage more syntactically.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.108
-Yeah. Oh, and do you debate?

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.877
You get in good debates
with the kids?

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:26.613
-Sure, all the time.
It's constant.

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:30.282
Well, I -- The problem
with married life

00:02:30.282 --> 00:02:34.120
I think for a man, for a
husband, is the amount of room

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.122
for you to complain
just gets smaller

00:02:37.122 --> 00:02:39.759
because it gets taken up
by the other people.

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.027
-Complaining, yeah.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.096
There's only so much
complaining that can happen.

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:45.097
-Yeah. You go, "Well --"
You know,

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:49.302
I'm lucky to get in
a, "Where's the remote?"

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.103
That's as much
as I can complain.

00:02:51.103 --> 00:02:53.672
-Yeah, because you've made
these humans and --

00:02:53.672 --> 00:02:56.709
-Yeah. "Maybe you could put it
back in the charger

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:57.776
when you're done with it."

00:02:57.776 --> 00:02:59.445
-You think?
-Yeah.

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:03.182
-Jessica sent out a very funny
post during quarantine.

00:03:03.182 --> 00:03:06.152 line:0%
It said that because
you have nowhere to perform,

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:08.254 line:0%
right now,
you walk around all day yelling.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.690 line:0%
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.292 line:0%
I think that that's accurate.

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:14.861
-Do you yell when you talk
in the house?

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:16.663
'Course you do.
-Yeah, you have to.

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:17.897
There's no one else.
-Yeah, you want to use

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:19.932
your stage voice,
'cause I'm not using it.

00:03:19.932 --> 00:03:22.501
I'm using it right now.
-Yep.

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:25.371
-But nobody wants
to hear this volume

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:28.942
for --
-For all day long.

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:30.442
-Are you done
with the maple syrup?

00:03:30.442 --> 00:03:33.880
Then put it back in the middle
of the table so I can reach it.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.317
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:38.618
-I just said that the other day,

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:40.586
'cause I do it --
I do it all the time.

00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:43.489
I want to talk about the new
book, "Is This Anything?"

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:44.757
"Is This Anything?"

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.525
-Yeah, I love
the way you said it,

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:49.395
'cause that is how we say it.
-Right?

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.363
That's the title.
-It's something you say. Yeah.

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:53.399
-You have an idea for a bit,
and you go,

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.134
"Hey, is this anything?"

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:57.370
[ Both laugh ]
-Yeah.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:00.839
-And what do we hope
that people say?

00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:03.442
-You hope they laugh,
but you have to say something.

00:04:03.442 --> 00:04:06.679
This says, "Here -- Bit coming.
Here comes a bit."

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:07.980
-Yes, that's true.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.448
-'Cause you can't
just go into it.

00:04:09.448 --> 00:04:11.284
Well, sometimes you can, but --

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:14.053
-Yeah, that's what I've been
trying to do with Nancy.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.723
I can't -- I have to say,
"I have an idea for a bit,"

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:18.624
and then I do it, because --
-Right.

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:20.826
-Yeah, because if I just
try to get into it,

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:22.227
it's too weird,
and it's like, "Wait,

00:04:22.227 --> 00:04:24.363
why are you talking about,
you know, golf or something?"

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:25.764
I don't know
what I was trying --

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:28.701
-Right, to Nancy, you would say,
"I have an idea for a bit."

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.003
To me, you would say,
"Is this anything?"

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:34.073
'cause I know all we're
interested in is bits anyway.

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.075
-Yeah, exactly right. Yeah.

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.210
-I wouldn't wonder
what "this" is.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.579
-Yeah. "Is this anything?"
means, yeah, is this a joke

00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:43.349
or is this somewhere
where I can try something?

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:45.518
-Yeah, can I get a laugh
with this somehow?

00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:48.921
-Uh, you know, I wanted to ask
when I'm looking at all these,

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.923
'cause, gosh, you go through
all the decades.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.092
It's awesome.
-Yeah, decade by decade.

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:55.995
Well, I turned 65,
and I thought,

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:58.097
"Well, that's the end
of regulation."

00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:00.132
I reached the end of regulation,

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:03.602
and I should turn something in.

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:04.970
You know, who knows?

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.538
-Who knows?
-Let's have it out there.

00:05:06.538 --> 00:05:07.940
Okay, this is what I did.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:10.509
Just wanted to make something
that I could leave and go,

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:13.679
"This is what I spent my life
doing, if you're interested."

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:17.216
-Yeah. Is this for comedians?

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:19.585
Is it just for fans of comedy?

00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:22.388
-Oh, anybody that likes comedy
I think would like it,

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:25.157
but I thought a lot
about comedians

00:05:25.157 --> 00:05:28.427
being able to look at it
and kind of figure out,

00:05:28.427 --> 00:05:31.064
"Oh, that's how he set
that joke up. I can --"

00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:33.065
So I did it in double space

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:35.434
so you can kind of analyze it
if you want.

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:37.937
"Well, how does he get
from that line to that line?"

00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:40.105
-That's -- I was going
to talk about this, I swear!

00:05:40.105 --> 00:05:41.507
-Really?
-I love that you did this.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.108
I've never seen it done before.

00:05:43.108 --> 00:05:45.711
Even in the first chapter,

00:05:45.711 --> 00:05:48.481
is you talking about the book.

00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:50.249
and you -- I've never seen --

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:53.619
Look at the spacing on this.
-Yeah, I double-spaced it.

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:56.321
-And I loved this
'cause I could do my own timing

00:05:56.321 --> 00:05:58.424
of reading this,
every single line.

00:05:58.424 --> 00:06:01.126
I've never seen it like that,
and it's set up, the whole book.

00:06:01.126 --> 00:06:02.761
It was brilliant.

00:06:02.761 --> 00:06:05.664
-Oh, I'm glad you liked it.
-I swear -- I wish I said it

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:08.467
before you said it,
because I really -- I loved it.

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:11.737
I go, "Oh, my gosh."
And I thought, blah, blah, blah.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:13.505
And I'm reading it
in the rhythm.

00:06:13.505 --> 00:06:15.641
And I go, "This is so easy
and fun to read,"

00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:17.443
before you get into
the material.

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:18.878
-Yeah.
-And then I go, "Oh."

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:22.448
-Well, good stand-up is really
like song lyrics or poetry.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:26.552
It's very kind of organized.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.554
It's a very organized
progression...

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:31.890
-Yeah. Here's the bridge.
-...like good music is.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.160
-Yeah. Here's the chorus.
Here's the bridge.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.262
-Yes.
-Here's the -- Yeah.

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:38.263
Do you look at any of these bits

00:06:38.263 --> 00:06:40.433
and go like, "I could make that
better right now.

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:42.968
I could punch that a little"?
-Yes. Yes, I do.

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:46.271
But I --

00:06:46.271 --> 00:06:49.308
You know, anybody could look
at their early work and go,

00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:53.746
"Oh, my gosh. This is not
as good as I got later on."

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:54.980
And I started doing that

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.149
when I was looking
at the earliest stuff.

00:06:57.149 --> 00:07:01.387
And then I thought, "That's not
really fair to your young self

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:04.123
who was --" 'Cause that stuff,

00:07:04.123 --> 00:07:07.993
whatever it is,
it got you to the next step,

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:10.395
which got to the next step
and the next step.

00:07:10.395 --> 00:07:15.034
So I thought, I'm going to be
nicer to this early stuff

00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:18.371
instead of going, "Oh, my gosh.
This is so infantile.

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:21.940
This is so basic. This is
so hacky," whatever it is.

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:26.012
But it got you there.
It got you from being just --

00:07:26.012 --> 00:07:28.514
I was just a kid in my room

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:30.816
to onstage
at Catch a Rising Star

00:07:30.816 --> 00:07:32.584
on First Avenue and 78th Street.

00:07:32.584 --> 00:07:35.020
-Yeah. I love that.
You set the stage so well,

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.857
like, sitting as a kid
watching the TV,

00:07:37.857 --> 00:07:40.426
eating cereal
in a horizontally striped shirt,

00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:42.561
I think I want to say.
And you're watching,

00:07:42.561 --> 00:07:44.897
you're going, "How can these
people talk like this?"

00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:46.632
You're watching
"Ed Sullivan Show."

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:48.701
And you go, "I'm going to see
if I can do that."

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:52.037
And you were eating a --
No, you're eating cereal.

00:07:52.037 --> 00:07:54.640
But then you said later
in that thing, you go,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.543
"I just want to do it
for laughs.

00:07:57.543 --> 00:07:59.177
That's really what I want
to do this for, but also,

00:07:59.177 --> 00:08:01.380
if I just have --
If I can make enough money

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.450
to buy Wonder Bread and Skippy
peanut butter, I'm set."

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:05.984
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:05.984 --> 00:08:10.222
-I thought, how funny do you
have to be to make enough

00:08:10.222 --> 00:08:11.891
to just get
a loaf of Wonder Bread

00:08:11.891 --> 00:08:13.759
and a jar of Skippy a week?"

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:16.529
'cause I could survive on that.

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:20.132
-When -- When was the last time
you had peanut butter on bread?

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:21.400
-Today.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.535
[ Both laugh ]

00:08:23.535 --> 00:08:24.737
-Was it good?

00:08:24.737 --> 00:08:26.205
-Yeah, it's good.
-Yeah, it's the best.

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:27.973
I don't know what they make
Wonder Bread out of,

00:08:27.973 --> 00:08:30.476
but wow. It is fantastic.
That's what makes it a wonder.

00:08:30.476 --> 00:08:32.277
-It's not bread.
It's better than bread.

00:08:32.277 --> 00:08:35.681
-It's more wonder.
It's more wonder than bread.

00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:38.417
-Yeah. You wonder.

00:08:38.417 --> 00:08:39.885
-You wonder, "What the heck
am I eating?"

00:08:39.885 --> 00:08:42.087
-You wonder, "What is this?"
[ Both laugh ]

00:08:42.087 --> 00:08:43.388
There you go. That's something.

00:08:43.388 --> 00:08:45.090
-That's something.
I'm telling you.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.960
Here's you in the Comic Strip,
which I loved.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:49.361
-Yeah.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:53.065
-Do you remember your first
night, and were you nervous?

00:08:53.065 --> 00:08:55.667
Were you --
Is it that butterflies?

00:08:55.667 --> 00:08:57.803
Were you cocky?
What was it like the first --

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:59.705
-I was catatonically nervous.

00:08:59.705 --> 00:09:01.674
I didn't --
I didn't think I was funny.

00:09:01.674 --> 00:09:04.209
I didn't think -- I didn't know
if I was funny.

00:09:04.209 --> 00:09:07.813
My friends thought I was funny,
but I was not convinced of it.

00:09:07.813 --> 00:09:08.847
-Yeah.

00:09:08.847 --> 00:09:11.684
-And so my friend told me

00:09:11.684 --> 00:09:15.154
that Humphrey Bogart would
always have a shot of Scotch

00:09:15.154 --> 00:09:17.389
before he would do
something difficult.

00:09:17.389 --> 00:09:20.759
So we went to the bar next door
to Catch a Rising Star,

00:09:20.759 --> 00:09:23.229
and we threw down
a shot of Scotch.

00:09:23.229 --> 00:09:25.097
-Did that help?
-No.

00:09:25.097 --> 00:09:27.966
-No.
[ Both laugh ]

00:09:27.966 --> 00:09:29.367
No, of course not.
It did nothing.

00:09:29.367 --> 00:09:30.569
You're just starting out.

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:33.672
I think that's a thing
about stand-up, too.

00:09:33.672 --> 00:09:36.308
It's like, even -- even now
when you do a joke,

00:09:36.308 --> 00:09:37.943
I don't know if I'm funny.

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:39.344
-You don't know. You're not.

00:09:39.344 --> 00:09:42.047
If the joke's not funny,
you're not in that moment.

00:09:42.047 --> 00:09:43.315
-Yeah.
-In that moment

00:09:43.315 --> 00:09:45.918
you're -- you're
absolutely nothing

00:09:45.918 --> 00:09:48.120
of what you want to be.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:51.090
-If you get a laugh, you're
everything that you want to be.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.191
-It's the best feeling
in the world.

00:09:53.191 --> 00:09:55.661
-Yeah. And the worst.

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.930
-And the worst if you don't,
-If you don't.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:59.999
-Yeah, that's --
-And that's what people

00:09:59.999 --> 00:10:02.367
I think are attracted to it for.

00:10:02.367 --> 00:10:05.170
They want to see a person --
That's why I watch baseball.

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:06.371
Is this guy going to get a hit,

00:10:06.371 --> 00:10:08.974
or is he going to have to
trudge back to the dugout

00:10:08.974 --> 00:10:10.909
with his bat, just holding it,

00:10:10.909 --> 00:10:13.412
going, "I really
didn't even use this"?

00:10:13.412 --> 00:10:15.615
[ Both laugh ]

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:20.820
What, you schvitzing?
-Yeah.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:23.789
I'm -- No, it's the new --
It's called dewy now.

00:10:23.789 --> 00:10:25.958
-You know,
whenever I come on your show,

